    "winner": "a-petrelli",
    "clashSummary": "JM vs A. PETRELLI took place at **Ministry of Sound** during a PenGame Rap Battles event powered by SoundOn. The host introduces the battle as one with strong potential, even suggesting it could be battle of the night. PETRELLI is welcomed back to the platform, and the crowd gives him a strong reception before the battle begins.\n\nJM goes first.\n\nJM opens round one by saying he wanted a rematch with SMIL3Z, but instead he has been given PETRELLI. He attacks PETRELLI’s appearance, weight, comeback, courage, family and lack of growth. He calls him skinny, malnourished, lacking courage, and says PETRELLI came back but is hardly a threat. The round has a clear battle-rap structure and several direct shots, but some of it feels more like standard insult work than a full character breakdown.\n\nPETRELLI responds with one of the stronger first-round counters of the battle. He immediately challenges JM’s gangster image, saying none of the violence bars are real and asking how JM can come to the South East and claim real drill. He attacks JM’s Welsh background, farm imagery, sheep jokes, fatherhood, daughter-protection angle and white-gangster image. His round has more identity and sharper direction than JM’s opener.\n\nJM’s second round is more aggressive. He says PETRELLI is not top five and not worth sizing up, attacks his family, mother, money, looks, body, drug image and music status. He brings in cockroach imagery, crack-smoker comparisons, Louis Theroux documentary references and says PETRELLI looks like a run-gun. It is a solid round with more bite than his first, but PETRELLI’s second becomes the round that defines the battle.\n\nPETRELLI’s second round is the standout moment. He switches into a Yardman / patois-heavy flow, and the room reacts immediately. The host and crowd want it brought back, and even though PETRELLI briefly looks to the crowd for a reload and drops the mic moment, the section has already taken over the battle. His delivery, rhythm and confidence give the round the biggest live impact. The judges later specifically identify PETRELLI’s second as the round that carried the decision.\n\nJM’s third round is a strong closer. He attacks PETRELLI as skinny, light, irrelevant, on benefits, lacking presence, and says he has nothing to gain from battling him. He uses chess/checkmate, screw loose, Dead Space, voodoo, trellis, petroleum jelly, ladder/rank imagery, revenge for a previous loss, blindness/white references and ends with a strong “intense pain” closer. It is probably JM’s best complete round.\n\nPETRELLI closes with a more direct and personal third. He uses the repeated “Joseph, please” refrain, then moves into biblical material around Moses, Joseph and the jacket. He attacks JM’s family, father, sister, home life, lack of “yard gyal,” Cardiff background, Jamaican identity and status. The round is more opponent-specific than JM’s third and brings the battle back to personal attack rather than just flow.\n\nAfter the battle, the judges deliberate.\n\nProfessor Green praises JM’s flow and the way he puts material together, but says PETRELLI clearly took the second round and ultimately gives the battle to PETRELLI. Abra Kadabra says JM needed to diss PETRELLI more and that PETRELLI “stole it.” Harry Pinero agrees that PETRELLI’s second round was the best round and says PETRELLI has to win.\n\nOfficial winner: **PETRELLI**.",
